WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:03.760
This is a brine shrimp.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.960
As it moves through the water it
creates swirling eddies behind it.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:14.220
A single shrimp only moves a tiny 
amount of water. But in a large group,

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:19.760
could creatures like these move enough 
water to influence the mixing of an ocean?

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:31.240
To find out, research has kept groups of
brine shrimp in tall tanks in their lab.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:36.660
Then they suspended tiny particles in the  water
which they could track to analyze its flow.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:41.920
By shining a light from the top of the tank 
they could cause the swarm to swim up,

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.500
mimicking the natural migratory 
behaviour of other shrimp like krill.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.840
And as they rose something
surprising happened.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:57.800
Together the shrimp produced a jet of
water as large as the swarm itself,

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:00.800
forcing water down as they swam up.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:07.220
A decade ago scientists didn't think this 
kind of aggregation effect was possible,

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:09.900
but this research shows that it can happen

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:14.940
and according to the researchers
on a large enough scale to impact the climate.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:20.080
You see brine shrimp are only a model
organism but the researchers think

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.310
that this phenomenon might also happen
with critters like krill, which live in

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:28.580
climatically sensitive areas like the
Southern Ocean.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:33.660
Water jets on the scale of swarms of krill,
which can get large enough to see from space,

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.520
could have major impacts 
on ocean mixing.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.920
Changing the balance of ocean chemistry,
nutrient flows and even the climate.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.780
Now this effect has only been seen in a lab

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:49.160
and predictions about its impacts
are mostly speculation

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.500
But if confirmed in field studies it could
represent an entirely new role played by

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.320
some of the most abundant animals on the planet.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:03.520
Not to mention oceanographers would
have a brand new variable to add to their models.

